; M1f S1f.5.21 — `eq` at one argument, written in a macro's body.
;
; Written in a rule, `(eq ?x)` is a parse error: the grammar's `Guard` takes two
; terms. A macro's body is a template, whose head may be any name at any arity
; (`ein.y`'s `Template`), since a macro's parameters stand for whole premises, so
; the grammar never reads the expanded rule and the compiler is the first to
; see `(eq ?x)`. It refuses it where it compiles `r`, on every command that
; compiles, with one line: the place in the macro's body, the rule, the form and
; what to write instead. The views and `kb save` accept the program, since none
; of them executes a rule; where this is refused is S1f.5.24's, on the user's
; ruling on Q-M1f.67 (an unused malformed macro loads, and no path executes the
; primitive).
;
; The one compile refusal a corpus program reaches since S1f.5.21, which moved
; the others to the loader (`nested_or.ein`, now under `../load/`). Until then
; this line was the Python IR's: `` `(eq …)` takes exactly 2 arguments and was
; given 1: SForm(head=Atom(name='eq'), args=(Var(name='x'),)) at Loc(file=…,
; line=…, col=…) — … ``, behind `ein.inference.compile.CompileError:` on
; `ein saturate` and the file's name on `ein render kb`.
(relation q T :why "q holds of {?1}")
(macro one (?a) (eq ?a) :why "{?a} equals")
(rule r () :match (and (q ?x) (one ?x)) :assert (q ?x) :why "{?x} is a q that equals, so a q")
(q a)
